  "irVersion": 8,
  "notes": {
    "text-plan": "Decides how types are written in the adapter contract, which notes a query carries, and which sections the contract has.",
    "text-plan.calcs.notes": "The notes a query of the outside world carries: its promise if it has one, then may-fail if it may fail.",
    "text-plan.calcs.sections": "Which sections the contract has, from how many commands answer, how many queries and routes there are, whether the system restarts, how many sorts of atoms there are, and whether JSON values appear: the answers of commands when some command answers; queries when there is one; routes when there is one; restarts when the system restarts; the sorts when there is one; the JSON type when JSON values appear.",
    "text-plan.calcs.typeText": "How one type is written, given what is inside it already written. An integer is number, a string string, a boolean boolean, JSON is Json. An enumeration is all its members (however many there are) joined by \" | \" (never when it has none). An atom is the name of its sort. An optional value is what is inside, followed by \" | null\". A list and a set are what is inside followed by \"[]\"; what is inside is put in parentheses when it contains \"|\" or \"&\" and does not start with \"{\". A map is \"{ key: K; value: V }[]\" with the key (key) and the value (inner). A record is \"{ \", its fields as \"name: text\" (all of them, however many there are) joined by \"; \", then \" }\" (a name that is not plain is written as a JSON string), or Record<string, never> when it has no field."
  },
